1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,430
Open the settings on almost any phone,
and you can find a list like this one.

2
00:00:05,810 --> 00:00:09,280
Music, about twelve dollars a month.

3
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:09,790
Streaming, fifteen.

4
00:00:09,790 --> 00:00:11,830
Extra cloud storage, three.

5
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:13,670
A fitness app, thirteen.

6
00:00:13,670 --> 00:00:15,180
A productivity app, ten.

7
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:17,550
And an AI assistant, twenty.

8
00:00:17,930 --> 00:00:20,530
None of them feels expensive on its own.

9
00:00:20,530 --> 00:00:23,710
Together, that is about
seventy-three dollars a month,

10
00:00:23,710 --> 00:00:27,330
or roughly eight hundred
and eighty dollars a year.

11
00:00:27,710 --> 00:00:31,810
This bundle is a made-up
example, not anyone's real bill.

12
00:00:31,810 --> 00:00:34,110
But the shift behind it is real.

13
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:41,370
In January 2022, about 2,000 new subscription
apps launched in a single month.

14
00:00:41,370 --> 00:00:46,380
By January 2026, it was
more than 14,700 a month.

15
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:50,210
So why does every app suddenly
want a monthly payment?

16
00:00:50,210 --> 00:00:52,230
And who actually comes out ahead?

17
00:00:53,130 --> 00:00:56,900
Not long ago, buying software
looked a lot like buying a book.

18
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:02,000
You paid once, you got a box
with a disc, or a download code,

19
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,780
and that version was yours to keep using
for as long as your computer could run it.

20
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:08,820
When a new version came out,

21
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:12,400
you decided whether the new features
were worth paying for again.

22
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:14,770
That was never the whole story.

23
00:01:14,770 --> 00:01:18,930
Businesses often paid yearly
fees for support and updates.

24
00:01:18,930 --> 00:01:20,490
Oracle, for example,

25
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:24,340
says that substantially all
of its customers buy and

26
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:27,400
renew their software support
contracts every year.

27
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:32,240
But for many everyday products,
one purchase was the deal.

28
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:37,529
Then, in May 2013, one of the best-known
software makers changed the rules.

29
00:01:37,909 --> 00:01:44,479
Adobe announced that new features for its creative
tools would go only to Creative Cloud subscribers,

30
00:01:44,479 --> 00:01:47,969
and that Creative Suite
6 would be the last major

31
00:01:47,969 --> 00:01:51,379
update for people who had
bought perpetual licenses.

32
00:01:52,279 --> 00:01:54,049
In the short run, the switch hurt.

33
00:01:54,429 --> 00:01:59,989
In fiscal 2012, subscriptions were
15 percent of Adobe's revenue.

34
00:01:59,989 --> 00:02:05,709
A year later they were 28 percent, and
total revenue fell about 8 percent,

35
00:02:05,709 --> 00:02:10,149
from $4.4 billion to $4.06 billion.

36
00:02:10,529 --> 00:02:16,379
Net income fell from $833
million to $290 million.

37
00:02:16,759 --> 00:02:17,929
Why accept that?

38
00:02:17,929 --> 00:02:22,889
Because a box is sold once, but a
subscription is billed again and again.

39
00:02:22,889 --> 00:02:27,949
Revenue that used to arrive in one
burst is spread across months and years.

40
00:02:28,329 --> 00:02:33,369
By fiscal 2014, subscriptions
were half of Adobe's revenue.

41
00:02:33,369 --> 00:02:39,711
In fiscal 2025, they were 96
percent of $23.8 billion.

42
00:02:40,091 --> 00:02:43,651
Not all of that growth came from
the subscription switch alone.

43
00:02:43,651 --> 00:02:48,681
But almost all of Adobe's revenue now
comes from customers who keep paying.

44
00:02:49,581 --> 00:02:52,931
To see why, imagine two
small software companies.

45
00:02:52,931 --> 00:02:54,661
Both are hypothetical.

46
00:02:55,041 --> 00:02:59,611
The first sells a photo
editor for $120, once.

47
00:02:59,611 --> 00:03:03,361
The second sells a similar
app for $10 a month.

48
00:03:03,741 --> 00:03:07,241
In month one, the first
company looks like the winner.

49
00:03:07,241 --> 00:03:12,361
A thousand customers
bring in $120,000 at once.

50
00:03:12,361 --> 00:03:14,641
The second company collects $10,000.

51
00:03:15,021 --> 00:03:19,371
But next month, the first company
has to find a thousand new buyers.

52
00:03:19,371 --> 00:03:23,405
The second starts the month with
the customers it already has.

53
00:03:23,785 --> 00:03:27,315
If all of them stay for a year,
the second company catches up.

54
00:03:27,315 --> 00:03:30,595
And it knows, roughly, what
next month will bring.

55
00:03:30,975 --> 00:03:33,395
That is the attraction: predictability.

56
00:03:33,395 --> 00:03:37,385
Recurring revenue is easier to
forecast and to plan around,

57
00:03:37,385 --> 00:03:42,975
and it can pay for work that never really
ends: fixing bugs, supporting new phones,

58
00:03:42,975 --> 00:03:44,275
running servers.

59
00:03:44,655 --> 00:03:48,365
Some products also cost money
every time you use them.

60
00:03:48,365 --> 00:03:50,605
Files have to be stored somewhere.

61
00:03:50,605 --> 00:03:53,485
An AI answer has to be computed.

62
00:03:53,485 --> 00:03:57,295
A single payment does not cover
a cost that keeps coming back.

63
00:03:57,675 --> 00:04:00,595
But notice the catch in
the second company's plan.

64
00:04:00,595 --> 00:04:03,215
It only works if those customers stay.

65
00:04:04,115 --> 00:04:08,815
Subscription businesses live by a
handful of numbers, and they all connect.

66
00:04:09,195 --> 00:04:12,495
Monthly recurring revenue
is what the company expects

67
00:04:12,495 --> 00:04:15,553
to bill each month from
its active subscribers.

68
00:04:15,933 --> 00:04:19,613
Churn is the share of subscribers
who leave in a given period.

69
00:04:19,613 --> 00:04:23,493
Retention is the opposite: the
share who are still paying.

70
00:04:23,873 --> 00:04:28,573
Customer acquisition cost is what the
company spends, on ads and promotion,

71
00:04:28,573 --> 00:04:31,453
to win one paying customer.

72
00:04:31,453 --> 00:04:34,393
And lifetime value is
what that customer is

73
00:04:34,393 --> 00:04:36,893
worth over all the
months they keep paying.

74
00:04:37,273 --> 00:04:39,553
Let's put hypothetical
numbers on our ten-dollar app.

75
00:04:39,553 --> 00:04:42,453
If it sells through Apple's App Store,

76
00:04:42,453 --> 00:04:48,983
Apple's terms give the developer 70 percent of
each payment during a subscriber's first year,

77
00:04:48,983 --> 00:04:51,853
and 85 percent after that.

78
00:04:51,853 --> 00:04:57,223
Developers in Apple's Small Business
Program get 85 percent from the start.

79
00:04:57,603 --> 00:05:01,023
So the developer keeps about $7 a month.

80
00:05:01,023 --> 00:05:04,543
If 10 percent of subscribers
cancel every month,

81
00:05:04,543 --> 00:05:09,503
the average subscriber stays about
ten months, and brings in roughly $70.

82
00:05:09,883 --> 00:05:13,953
If winning each subscriber
costs $40 in advertising,

83
00:05:13,953 --> 00:05:16,069
that looks like a healthy business.

84
00:05:16,449 --> 00:05:18,989
But if churn doubles
to 20 percent a month,

85
00:05:18,989 --> 00:05:22,519
the average subscriber
brings in about $35.

86
00:05:22,519 --> 00:05:25,739
That is less than it cost to win them.

87
00:05:25,739 --> 00:05:30,609
Same app, same price, and now
every new customer loses money.

88
00:05:30,989 --> 00:05:34,009
That is why the people who
run subscription apps watch

89
00:05:34,009 --> 00:05:37,089
churn more closely than
almost anything else.

90
00:05:37,989 --> 00:05:41,809
So how do real subscription
apps actually do?

91
00:05:41,809 --> 00:05:44,729
One of the largest data
sets comes from RevenueCat,

92
00:05:44,729 --> 00:05:50,289
a company that sells the software many
apps use to manage their subscriptions.

93
00:05:50,669 --> 00:05:58,929
Its 2026 report covers more than 115,000
apps and more than $16 billion in revenue.

94
00:05:58,929 --> 00:06:03,909
It is not every app in the world, only
apps that use RevenueCat's tools.

95
00:06:03,909 --> 00:06:05,819
But the picture is striking.

96
00:06:06,199 --> 00:06:11,879
One year after launch, the median
app earned about $72 a month.

97
00:06:11,879 --> 00:06:15,799
The top 10 percent earned
more than $2,500 a month.

98
00:06:16,179 --> 00:06:19,639
Only 4.6 percent of newly
launched apps reached

99
00:06:19,639 --> 00:06:23,039
$10,000 in monthly
revenue within two years.

100
00:06:23,039 --> 00:06:27,999
And apps launched before
2020 still generate 69

101
00:06:27,999 --> 00:06:31,399
percent of all subscription
revenue in the data.

102
00:06:31,779 --> 00:06:34,119
Keeping subscribers is even harder.

103
00:06:34,119 --> 00:06:37,279
For subscriptions that started in 2024,

104
00:06:37,279 --> 00:06:42,744
the median share still active a year later
was about 8 percent on monthly plans,

105
00:06:42,744 --> 00:06:45,809
and 28 percent on yearly plans.

106
00:06:46,189 --> 00:06:50,275
Nearly half of monthly subscribers
do not renew even once.

107
00:06:50,655 --> 00:06:55,415
Those figures count subscriptions,
not people, and payments, not use.

108
00:06:55,415 --> 00:06:58,745
Someone can cancel and
keep using a free version.

109
00:06:58,745 --> 00:07:02,153
Someone else can keep paying
and barely open the app.

110
00:07:03,053 --> 00:07:05,203
Now look at it from the customer's side.

111
00:07:05,583 --> 00:07:08,403
A household's monthly budget is finite.

112
00:07:08,403 --> 00:07:13,823
Every new subscription has to find room in
it, often by pushing something else out.

113
00:07:14,203 --> 00:07:17,523
So apps are not only
competing with similar apps.

114
00:07:17,523 --> 00:07:20,823
A meditation app competes
with a streaming service,

115
00:07:20,823 --> 00:07:24,833
and an AI assistant competes
with extra cloud storage,

116
00:07:24,833 --> 00:07:27,213
for the same few dollars each month.

117
00:07:27,593 --> 00:07:32,113
That is why so much effort goes into
the first moments: free trials,

118
00:07:32,113 --> 00:07:35,603
discounted first months,
cheaper yearly plans.

119
00:07:35,603 --> 00:07:40,093
Each is a way to get onto the list,
and to survive the first renewal.

120
00:07:40,473 --> 00:07:45,303
RevenueCat found that apps asking for
payment upfront, behind a hard paywall,

121
00:07:45,303 --> 00:07:51,873
turned about five times as many downloads
into paying customers as freemium apps did:

122
00:07:51,873 --> 00:07:56,673
10.7 percent versus 2.1
percent within 35 days.

123
00:07:56,673 --> 00:08:00,593
Yet a year later, retention
was nearly the same.

124
00:08:00,973 --> 00:08:04,253
Meanwhile, small payments add up quietly.

125
00:08:04,253 --> 00:08:08,613
Our hypothetical bundle costs
less than three dollars a day.

126
00:08:08,613 --> 00:08:11,023
Over a year, it comes
to nearly nine hundred.

127
00:08:11,403 --> 00:08:14,783
Not every app takes the
pure subscription road.

128
00:08:14,783 --> 00:08:16,123
In RevenueCat's data,

129
00:08:16,123 --> 00:08:18,623
nearly a quarter of the apps pair their

130
00:08:18,623 --> 00:08:20,563
subscriptions with a one-time lifetime

131
00:08:20,563 --> 00:08:21,729
purchase.

132
00:08:22,629 --> 00:08:24,699
Recurring revenue sounds safe.

133
00:08:24,699 --> 00:08:28,559
It is not guaranteed, and it
is not the same as profit.

134
00:08:28,939 --> 00:08:30,619
Payments fail.

135
00:08:30,619 --> 00:08:32,139
Cards expire.

136
00:08:32,139 --> 00:08:34,139
Banks decline charges.

137
00:08:34,139 --> 00:08:35,439
In RevenueCat's data,

138
00:08:35,439 --> 00:08:41,719
involuntary billing failures caused about 31 percent
of subscription cancellations on Google Play,

139
00:08:41,719 --> 00:08:44,929
compared with 14 percent on the App Store.

140
00:08:45,309 --> 00:08:46,949
Hype fades.

141
00:08:46,949 --> 00:08:51,789
AI-powered apps in the report earned
41 percent more revenue per payer,

142
00:08:51,789 --> 00:08:55,379
but their subscribers churned
about 30 percent faster.

143
00:08:55,759 --> 00:09:01,259
Some companies try a shortcut: make
it easy to sign up and hard to leave.

144
00:09:01,259 --> 00:09:02,899
That can backfire.

145
00:09:02,899 --> 00:09:05,059
In September 2025,

146
00:09:05,059 --> 00:09:10,519
Amazon agreed to pay $2.5 billion to settle
Federal Trade Commission claims that it

147
00:09:10,519 --> 00:09:15,729
enrolled customers in Prime without their
consent and made cancelling difficult.

148
00:09:16,109 --> 00:09:19,009
And revenue is not profit.

149
00:09:19,009 --> 00:09:22,589
Spotify, a streaming service
rather than a typical app,

150
00:09:22,589 --> 00:09:30,609
ended 2025 with 290 million premium
subscribers and €17.2 billion in revenue.

151
00:09:30,989 --> 00:09:33,869
Most of that money goes straight back out,

152
00:09:33,869 --> 00:09:37,029
mainly as royalties
and distribution costs.

153
00:09:37,029 --> 00:09:40,049
Its gross margin was 32 percent,

154
00:09:40,049 --> 00:09:47,373
and as recently as 2023 it reported
an operating loss of €446 million.

155
00:09:47,753 --> 00:09:52,233
In 2025, its operating
income was €2.2 billion.

156
00:09:52,233 --> 00:09:56,293
Subscriptions can become very profitable,

157
00:09:56,293 --> 00:09:59,653
but only when the costs behind
them are under control.

158
00:10:00,553 --> 00:10:03,033
So why does every app want a subscription?

159
00:10:03,413 --> 00:10:07,513
Because a subscription turns a
single sale into a relationship:

160
00:10:07,513 --> 00:10:10,423
one that pays every
month, can be forecast,

161
00:10:10,423 --> 00:10:13,513
and can fund the work of
keeping a product alive.

162
00:10:13,893 --> 00:10:16,113
For customers, that can be a good deal.

163
00:10:16,113 --> 00:10:19,663
You pay less upfront, you
get updates and new features,

164
00:10:19,663 --> 00:10:22,693
and you can leave when the
product stops being worth it.

165
00:10:23,073 --> 00:10:25,313
It can also be a worse deal.

166
00:10:25,313 --> 00:10:30,273
If you use a tool rarely, or would
happily keep an older version forever,

167
00:10:30,273 --> 00:10:33,313
owning it outright can cost far less.

168
00:10:33,313 --> 00:10:36,523
That is why one-time purchases
have not disappeared.

169
00:10:36,523 --> 00:10:40,993
Even Adobe still licenses perpetual
versions of some products.

170
00:10:41,373 --> 00:10:43,983
The data points to something easy to miss.

171
00:10:43,983 --> 00:10:46,413
Most subscriptions do not last.

172
00:10:46,413 --> 00:10:48,823
Most apps never make much money.

173
00:10:48,823 --> 00:10:52,573
The few that win are the ones
people decide, month after month,

174
00:10:52,573 --> 00:10:54,463
are still worth paying for.

175
00:10:54,843 --> 00:10:57,543
Companies would like that
payment to be automatic.

176
00:10:57,543 --> 00:11:01,453
The best ones still have to
earn it, every single month.

177
00:11:01,833 --> 00:11:03,393
This is Hidden Ledger.

178
00:11:03,393 --> 00:11:06,393
Every number in this video is
sourced in the description.

